th now but  prices would still be going up and if  you're on the red line that's still  getting harder for you indeed so I think  the uh I think inflation will start to  come down uh but just how much and when  I think I'm not brave enough to forecast  that makes sense  um just the final sort of forecast  question if you like and it was just a  soft one don't worry  um the economic growth thickers for  November which were released this week  we're actually surprising a lot better  than some people expected  um do you think that we should have a  Slither of optimism I guess that the  landing may be softer than we think well  I think a sliver but you know individual  months figures can be first of all they  get adjusted subsequently they don't  always end up the same as they start the  World Cup may have helped people were  you know buying large amounts of well I  was staying in and buying large amounts  of beer or whatever to to celebrate so I  wouldn't read too much into uh these  figures but one's hope would be that if  we do have a recession that it's a  relatively mild one and not too  prolonged but you know that's a hope  rather than the forecast we um have  talked a lot about inflation and what it  means to people's budgeting obviously  the big worry is that people's pay isn't  keeping up with inflation what's Tesco's  policy been when it comes to pay well I  think we had our normal pay settlement  in the you know first second quarter of  2022 we then realized it was racing  Rising inflation that wasn't adequate so  we had a second round negotiations with  our unions and our colleagues and we  ended up I think with a overall rise for  our colleagues on the shop floor  um of around eight percent last year and  we've told them that we will start this  year's negotiations earlier in fact in  the next couple of weeks so that if it  is necessary to make a further  adjustment and I don't want to  anticipate that we will be in a position  to do so so I think we think 